WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.760
Every day, it seems,

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:04.280
there's a new, bewildering
or frightening story

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:07.840
about AI in the news -
how it's going to steal our jobs,

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:11.080
spread internet fakery
on a colossal scale

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:13.600
and generally take over the world.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:17.160
But what exactly is AI -
artificial intelligence -

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.840
and are the scare stories even true?

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:32.760
So, the first thing
to know is that AI has been around

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.640
a lot longer than you might think.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:39.920
Its roots lie in an idea known
as an "artificial neural network"

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.800
from the 1940s.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:45.280
A neural network is a bit like
a team of interconnected workers

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:47.000
that learn to solve problems.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.200
Each time they come up with
a possible solution, it's marked.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.040
If there is room for improvement,

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.240
they adjust and change
their connections.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:57.480
Over time the network
becomes more efficient.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:02.920
And technology powered by neural
networks is all around us right now.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.080
It suggests movies and music
we might like.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:09.040
It recognises faces and objects
when taking photos on smartphones,

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:12.160
enabling features
like facial recognition.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:17.000
It's heavily used by social media
platforms to personalise our feeds.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.840
More recently, a form of AI
known as "generative AI"

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.320
is powering applications that
can seemingly create new data.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:30.160
It can also power chatbots like
Open AI's ChatGPT and Google's Bard,

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.640
which give humanlike responses
to questions.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:35.840
These are getting better
at interacting with us,

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.200
and seemingly more humanlike.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:41.720
This can seem scary,
but it's worth knowing point two.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:49.280
If you ask ChatGPT
a question like this one -

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:52.040
"Why should we be concerned
about AI?" -

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:54.760
it does a pretty good job
of providing a response

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:56.720
that appears logical.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.400
And with that convincing
humanlike response,

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.440
it's easy for us to believe
it understands what it's saying,

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:05.040
that it has feelings
and motivations.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:06.920
It's understandable
that we do this,

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.440
but it's worth remembering,
right now AI can't think or feel,

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:12.320
can't love or hate.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.400
ChatGPT and its counterparts

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:17.000
are sophisticated
sentence completion apps

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:19.600
that analyse our patterns
of communication

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:23.920
and provide responses similar to the
way humans would typically reply.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:26.160
A bit like a "talking" parrot.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.720
Which leads us
to point number three.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:36.560
Chatbots can have an
awkward relationship with the truth,

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.200
technically known
as "AI hallucinations".

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:42.400
You could also describe it
as "making stuff up".

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:45.680
The core of the technology
is a model that uses probability

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.960
to predict the next word,
sentence or paragraph.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:52.960
It can generate seemingly plausible
replies, but lacks the ability

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:57.080
to assess truthfulness
or the accuracy of its responses.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:00.520
So anyone thinking of using
chatbots to write content

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:01.760
needs to be careful

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.680
they're not incorporating
credible-sounding BS

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:07.040
that can be easily spotted
by someone

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.320
who's actually done the research.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:18.760
The idea of a racist machine
might seem far-fetched.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:23.160
But if AI is trained on data
that's racist, biased or hateful,

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.400
then its output will be too.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.640
As we all know, racism, bias
and hateful content can be found

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.960
in abundance online.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:35.440
In 2016, Microsoft launched
an experimental chatbot called Tay,

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:37.280
but quickly pulled the plug

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:40.120
after it made racist
and offensive remarks.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:42.640
It learnt to do this
from interacting with users

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:43.920
on social media.

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:45.240
Microsoft apologised

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:48.760
and promised to implement improved
safety features in future.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:52.720
This is why the ethical framework
that governs any AI application

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:56.280
is incredibly important, and why
many are calling for safeguards

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:00.480
to prevent bias and hate speech
to be built into AI systems.

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.440
For all the notes of caution,
it can be easy to forget

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:11.760
there's many
potential benefits to AI.

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:14.880
It's set to truly revolutionise
healthcare.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:16.920
AI has already discovered new drugs

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:19.320
and is being used to identify
cancer cells

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:21.440
much more reliably than humans.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:24.760
And AI chatbots can behave
like patient teachers

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:27.920
when we struggle to understand
a complex topic,

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:31.520
summarising huge volumes
of information for us.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:35.720
The AI revolution has the potential
to enhance and speed up work

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:39.600
in many fields, from software
programming, to animation,

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:42.440
to law enforcement and journalism.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.160
This has pluses and minuses,
of course,

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:49.080
but could this extra capacity
free us up to do other things?

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:50.760
Like tackling climate change

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:54.720
or looking after ourselves
and each other better?

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:57.880
As AI advances, governments
and regulators will of course

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:01.320
need to make sure it's being used
ethically and legally -

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.120
no easy feat.

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:05.800
But will AI take over the world?

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:08.280
Don't forget, AI is a tool,

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:12.040
and even a powerful tool can't
take over the world on its own.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.520
It's up to us to decide
how we use it -

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:17.320
or even if we should use it at all.

